Hello All Things Additive team,

a workflow story that runs from an imported file to a print-ready 3MF.

Solidon3D takes a downloaded model and recognises its features, so holes,
countersinks, threads, and now patterns such as honeycombs and knurls. Click a
hole and its dimensions appear on the model itself, type 8 instead of 6 and
the countersink follows, pick a fit for the screw, read the check report
before you export. Version 0.5.0 closes the gap that made the workflow
one-way: the result is now an exact body. Features stay exact through moving,
duplicating and removing, and STEP goes in and comes back out cleanly.

The downstream half is where it may fit your coverage. Filament colours stay
on exact bodies and follow every new meshing, so a multi-material part
survives editing. Fits are checked on the real bodies in their assembled
position, and the export can be cancelled before it runs. Exact bodies are
meshed for the target printer, for a resin device as finely as its pixels
require, and the check report names the value. The shape deviation map shows
which faces of a meshing lie how far from the original. Handover to the common
slicers is direct, and the file can also be opened in a resin vendor tool
whose settings Solidon does not know.

The check report is worth a line of its own, because it is the part that has
no equivalent upstream of a slicer: it names the cause. Which island in which
layer costs support material, and how far the part would have to be turned to
remove it, while the part can still be changed. Clearances work the same way,
entered as a reference to the material rather than as a number, with profiles
that can be calibrated against printed test pieces.

In this release the printing side also gained resin printers proper: two
generic devices by build volume, plus user-defined devices with pixel size and
minimum wall. A resin project no longer receives advice about nozzles, brims
or bridges.

Solidon3D is an independent development from Germany, desktop for Windows,
macOS and Linux, fully usable offline after a single activation, in six
languages. The demo is free and complete.

